---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="License and commercial use — DAIA" description="Build on DAIA under AGPL: commercial use is welcome, with reciprocal source-sharing obligations.">
    <main id="main" class="participation">
        <section class="participation-hero section-pad">
            <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to DAIA</a>
            <p class="eyebrow">Open-source software</p>
            <h1>Build on DAIA</h1>
            <p class="large-copy">You can use DAIA to run a paid service, build a business or organize a research project. We want useful work to spread, and improvements to the shared software to remain available to the people using it.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="participation-body section-pad">
            <h2>What the license asks in return</h2>
            <p>DAIA uses AGPL version 3 or later. If you modify DAIA and let people use that version over a network, you must prominently offer those users its corresponding source code at no charge. The license also sets conditions for distributing copies. It does not require you to pay DAIA or submit your changes to our repository.</p>
            <p>For example, a company can charge for hosting DAIA. If it improves DAIA’s scheduler, the users of that modified service must be offered the corresponding source. Other developers can then use those improvements under the same license.</p>
            <h2>An optional commercial license — not available yet</h2>
            <p>AGPL remains free to use, including for paid services. DAIA is exploring an alternative license for organizations that need to keep covered modifications proprietary or incorporate DAIA into a proprietary product. The complete official Core would remain available under AGPL, including project-controlled Core improvements offered commercially.</p>
            <p>The proposed licensor could be an identified company, including a founder-owned company, or an optional foundation. No licensor is appointed by this page. Reviewed agreements, contribution rights and clearance of the exact release are required first. Third-party licenses still apply. Support and managed hosting are separate possible services.</p>
            <h2>Contributors and funding</h2>
            <p>The proposed default lets contributors retain copyright while explicitly granting additional rights over accepted DAIA Core contributions. The legal participant, not the worker, must agree in advance. The agreement and acceptance workflow are not active yet; submitting a patch does not execute them.</p>
            <p>A company could fund development and pay for maintenance while also earning profit for its shareholders. That is different from independent nonprofit stewardship and must be disclosed before contributor agreement. No salary, dividend or contributor payment is created by this proposal. Separate funding and job agreements govern compensation; donating does not buy a license exception.</p>
            <p>Using DAIA does not automatically put a customer’s independent deliverables or data under AGPL or give DAIA rights in that work. Rights to work produced for a job must be agreed for that job. Incorporating DAIA code can create licensing obligations; their scope depends on how that code and the rest of the work are combined.</p>
            <p>The license permits forks and commercial competition. It cannot guarantee that every business will support DAIA financially, and it does not authorize anyone to present an independent service as the official DAIA project.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/joindaia/DAIA/blob/main/LICENSE">Read the full license ↗</a>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/joindaia/DAIA">Explore the source code ↗</a>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
